\begin{frontmatter}

\title{Implementation-Risk Differentials in Hydrogen Technology Pathways:\\
Evidence from a Segmented Investment Ecosystem and Carbon-Price-Conditional Risk}

\author[vu,gas]{Sake Saakstra\corref{cor1}}
\ead{s.saakstra@vu.nl}
\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author.}

\affiliation[vu]{organization={Student MSc Econometrics \& Operations Research, Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam},
                 country={Netherlands}}

\affiliation[gas]{organization={Gasunie, Business Line Waterstof Nederland},
                  city={Groningen},
                  country={Netherlands}}

\begin{abstract}
\noindent Hydrogen project realization is not primarily a comparison of two interchangeable technologies but a process governed by \textbf{structurally segmented investment ecosystems} within which implementation risk concentrates on \textbf{terminal cancellation rather than reversible delay} and is conditional on carbon-price exposure. Using the IEA Hydrogen Projects Database covering 2,380 announced projects globally, we develop this view through two primary contributions, supported by convergent empirical evidence and a mechanistic interpretation.

\smallskip\noindent\textit{Primary contribution 1 --- Segmented investment ecosystems.} $\BlueCCS$ and $\PEM$ projects operate in measurably distinct investment ecosystems: the propensity model achieves McFadden $R^2 = 0.70$; propensity score matching uses only 29 unique $\PEM$ controls for 147 $\BlueCCS$ matches; entropy balancing collapses to an effective sample size of 8. We argue that this non-exchangeability is substantive content rather than a methodological obstacle to reweight away.

\smallskip\noindent\textit{Primary contribution 2 --- Terminal cancellation, not reversible delay.} The Fine-Gray competing-risks decomposition reveals that the $\BlueCCS$ implementation-risk differential concentrates almost entirely on terminal cancellation ($\HR = 13.19\ [5.28, 32.91]$) rather than on real-option delay ($\HR = 1.20\ [0.34, 4.26]$, indistinguishable from one). Within the observed sample, $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to terminate rather than enter a pause-and-wait state.

\smallskip\noindent\textit{Supporting evidence --- convergence across estimators.} Within the comparable subset, eleven estimators across four classes of identifying assumptions yield $\BlueCCS$ hazard ratios consistently between 5 and 14 (preferred doubly robust $\HR = 6.87$; Cox PH $= 11.93\ [4.67, 30.49]$); proportional hazards is supported (Schoenfeld $p = 0.59$), sponsor frailty is absent ($\theta = 0.000$), and leave-one-region-out analyses confirm regional robustness across all seven regional exclusions ($p < 0.0001$ throughout). The contribution lies in the convergence of these independent signals toward a common qualitative pattern, not in precise effect-size identification, which 43 events would not support.

\smallskip\noindent\textit{Mechanism --- carbon-price-conditional risk.} The $\BlueCCS \times \EUA$ interaction coefficient is $-2.51$ ($p < 0.0001$), implying steeply declining predicted hazard ratios as carbon prices rise, with gas-price, VIX, and policy-uncertainty interactions all statistically insignificant. Predictions at low-carbon-price extrapolations should be read as directional stress-test indications rather than literal point forecasts.

\smallskip\noindent\textit{Conceptual interpretation.} The findings are consistent with fiscal support for blue hydrogen (the IRA 45V tax credit, EU REPowerEU support, the RFNBO Delegated Acts) being interpretable as transition-period insurance against low-carbon-price scenarios in which the supported technology would not survive to benefit from later recovery.
\end{abstract}

