% ============================================================
% 1. INTRODUCTION
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

Decarbonisation of energy systems requires building infrastructure that did not exist at scale a decade ago. Hydrogen produced via electrolysis is widely seen as a critical vector for hard-to-abate sectors including steel, ammonia, refining, and long-distance transport \citep{IEA2023, GlenkReichelstein2019}. The EU REPowerEU package \citep{EC2022REPowerEU} commits to 20 million tonnes of renewable hydrogen by 2030, the US Inflation Reduction Act \citep{IRA2022} provides up to \$3 per kilogram production tax credit via the 45V provision, and the EU Renewable Fuels of Non-Biological Origin (RFNBO) Delegated Acts \citep{EC2023RFNBO} codify the eligibility criteria for clean hydrogen. Yet a growing literature documents that announced infrastructure projects translate into operational facilities at low rates \citep{Flyvbjerg2014}, and the transition-finance literature has begun to quantify the implications of project failure for portfolio-level transition risk \citep{Mercure2018, BoltonKacperczyk2021, Caldecott2018}.

Within the hydrogen sector, a fundamental question concerns the relative reliability of two distinct technology pathways. Blue hydrogen --- produced from natural gas with carbon capture and storage (CCS) --- leverages existing fossil infrastructure and can in principle be deployed at scale rapidly \citep{Bui2018, Budinis2018}, but depends on functioning CCS capacity, faces carbon-price exposure, and confronts reputational risks. Green hydrogen via proton-exchange-membrane (PEM) electrolysis is purely electrical and pairs with renewable generation \citep{GlenkReichelstein2019}, but is currently more capital-intensive and requires substantial electrical infrastructure.

\subsection{Central thesis and contribution hierarchy}

This paper argues a single thesis: \textit{hydrogen project realisation is not primarily a comparison of two interchangeable technologies but a process governed by structurally segmented investment ecosystems within which implementation risk concentrates on terminal cancellation rather than reversible delay, and is conditional on carbon-price exposure}. We develop this view through two primary contributions, supported by convergent empirical evidence and a mechanistic interpretation.

\textbf{Primary contribution 1 --- Segmentation.} $\BlueCCS$ and $\PEM$ projects operate in measurably distinct investment ecosystems. The propensity score model that predicts technology choice from observable project characteristics achieves McFadden pseudo-$R^2 = 0.698$. Propensity score matching with $0.05$ caliper uses only 29 unique $\PEM$ controls to match 147 $\BlueCCS$ projects, and entropy balancing concentrates effective weight on just 8 of 470 $\PEM$ observations before diverging into non-identification. We treat this non-exchangeability as substantive content rather than a methodological problem to reweight away.

\textbf{Primary contribution 2 --- Terminal cancellation, not reversible delay.} The Fine-Gray competing-risks decomposition reveals that the $\BlueCCS$ implementation-risk differential is concentrated almost entirely on terminal cancellation ($\HR = 13.19$, 95\% CI $[5.28, 32.91]$, $p < 0.0001$) rather than on real-option delay ($\HR = 1.20$, 95\% CI $[0.34, 4.26]$, $p = 0.78$). Within the observed sample, $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to terminate rather than enter a pause-and-wait state in response to deteriorating conditions.

\textbf{Supporting evidence --- Convergent estimates across methods.} Within the comparable subset, eleven estimators across four classes of identifying assumptions yield $\BlueCCS$ hazard ratios consistently between 5 and 14, with leave-one-region-out analyses confirming the qualitative finding is regionally robust ($\HR$ range $[11.1, 19.9]$ across seven LOR specifications, $p < 0.0001$ throughout). The preferred doubly robust marginal estimate is $\HR = 6.87$; the Cox PH estimate is $\HR = 11.93$ $[4.67, 30.49]$. Proportional hazards is supported (Schoenfeld $p = 0.59$); sponsor frailty is absent ($\theta = 0.000$). The contribution here lies in \textit{convergence}: independent estimators with different identifying assumptions, different sampling structures, and different functional forms point in the same qualitative direction --- a stronger claim, given 43 events, than any single estimator's point estimate could support.

\textbf{Mechanism --- Carbon-price-conditional risk.} The $\BlueCCS \times \EUA$ carbon-price interaction coefficient is $-2.51$ ($p < 0.0001$). Delta-method marginal effects show that the predicted $\BlueCCS$ hazard ratio declines steeply as carbon prices rise. Interactions with gas prices, VIX, and economic policy uncertainty are statistically insignificant, sharpening the interpretation: it is not generalised macro stress that mechanistically drives the $\BlueCCS$ relative cancellation hazard, but specifically carbon-price exposure. Extreme predictions at very low carbon-price extrapolations are best read as directional stress-test indications rather than literal point forecasts.

\subsection{Contribution and scope}

The two primary contributions distinguish this paper from existing treatments of hydrogen technology comparison in two ways. First, methodologically: where prior work has compared blue versus green hydrogen failure rates with the implicit assumption that the two technologies represent comparable subpopulations, we document that the two populations are not exchangeable in any operational sense --- and that this non-exchangeability is itself substantive content. Second, substantively: the terminal-versus-delay decomposition combined with the carbon-price-conditional mechanism is interpretable, conceptually, as suggesting that fiscal support schemes for blue hydrogen (the IRA 45V tax credit, EU REPowerEU support, the RFNBO Delegated Acts) function less as cost-of-capital subsidies and more as transition-period insurance against low-carbon-price scenarios in which the supported technology would not survive to benefit from later carbon-price recovery. We position this as a conceptual interpretation of our findings, not as a normative policy conclusion --- the data structure does not support a full welfare or policy-optimisation analysis.

\subsection{Roadmap}

Section 2 describes the data. Section 3 documents the segmented-ecosystem structure (pillar 1). Section 4 outlines the empirical strategy across eleven estimators. Section 5 presents results across pillars 2--4. Section 6 reports additional robustness checks including leave-one-region-out analyses. Section 7 discusses identification, internal validity, and substantive implications. Section 8 concludes.

