\end{table}

The mechanism is rooted in the unit economics of carbon capture \citep{Bui2018, Budinis2018}. As the carbon price rises, captured $\mathrm{CO}_2$ becomes more economically valuable to retain rather than emit, and the CCS step in blue hydrogen production becomes economically advantageous rather than burdensome. The insignificance of interactions with gas prices, VIX, and EPU sharpens the interpretation: the $\BlueCCS$ relative hazard is not driven by generalised macro stress, but specifically by carbon-price exposure --- consistent with the transition-finance literature on technology-specific carbon-price sensitivity \citep{BoltonKacperczyk2021, Mercure2018}.

\textit{On reading the magnitudes.} The extreme hazard ratios at low carbon-price extrapolations (e.g.\ $\HR \approx 673$ at $z = -1$) follow logically from the linear-on-log-odds specification but rely on extrapolation outside the regions of densest observed support and across only 43 events. They are best interpreted as \textit{directional stress-test indications} of how a low-carbon-price environment would have stressed $\BlueCCS$ implementation viability, not as literal point forecasts of the conditional cancellation hazard at a specific future $\EUA$ level. What can be reliably read from the curve is its qualitative shape --- a steep, statistically significant monotonic decline in the predicted $\BlueCCS$ relative hazard as carbon prices rise. The 95\% CI at $z = +1$ excludes $\HR = 1$, confirming that the differential persists at currently observed European ETS prices. The 95\% CI at $z = -1$ is so wide that its lower bound ($\HR \approx 215$) is still very large and its upper bound is best treated as an indication of the model's uncertainty rather than a literal upper-end forecast.

\subsection{Competing risks: terminal cancellation versus real-option delay}

The Fine-Gray subdistribution hazard framework (Equation~\ref{eq:fg}) distinguishes \textit{Plans cancelled} (event 1, terminal failure) from \textit{On-hold confirmed} (event 2, real-option delay in the sense of \citet{DixitPindyck1994}). Of the 43 events, 31 are terminal cancellations and 12 are on-hold transitions. The $\BlueCCS$ implementation-risk differential is concentrated almost entirely on terminal cancellation ($\HR = 13.19$, 95\% CI $[5.28, 32.91]$) and is statistically indistinguishable from one for on-hold transitions ($\HR = 1.20$, 95\% CI $[0.34, 4.26]$, $p = 0.78$). Within the observed sample, $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to terminate rather than enter a pause-and-wait state.

The interpretation is consistent with the view that fiscal instruments designed to support blue hydrogen function less as one-time cost-of-capital subsidies and more as continuous transition-period insurance: once a project enters the cancellation pathway, it does not appear to reverse within our sample. Combined with the carbon-price interaction, the pattern suggests that at low carbon prices, $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to terminate rather than wait for recovery --- although the small number of events (12 on-hold transitions in total) warrants caution before extrapolating this as a structural absence of real-option behaviour.

\subsection{Sponsor frailty}

The shared frailty Cox model (Equation~\ref{eq:frailty}) with gamma-distributed random effects on sponsor estimates a frailty variance $\hat\theta = 0.000$, indicating negligible within-sponsor-type heterogeneity beyond what the fixed sponsor type indicator captures. The $\BlueCCS$ hazard ratio under frailty correction is $13.73$, identical to the Cox baseline. With 256 unique sponsors and only 21 sponsors experiencing events, individual-sponsor random effects cannot be identified. Methodologically, this confirms that sponsor type as a fixed effect is a sufficient specification.

% ============================================================
% 6. ROBUSTNESS
% ============================================================
\section{Robustness}

\subsection{Sensitivity to caliper and trimming}

Tighter PSM calipers (0.02, 0.01) yield similar point estimates. IPW with trimming at $[0.05, 0.95]$ is the primary IPW specification.

\subsection{Sponsor type aggregation}

The DR estimator aggregates sponsor types into three groups to avoid separation; re-running with the original eight-level categorisation under Firth penalisation yields qualitatively similar results. The frailty Cox result ($\theta = 0.000$) validates this aggregation.

\subsection{Fine-Gray specification sensitivity}

The Fine-Gray specification uses a reduced covariate set ($\BlueCCS$ $+$ log capacity) because the full specification with sponsor type and region dummies produces a singular design matrix given 31 cancelled and 12 on-hold events. Adding sponsor type one level at a time does not change the qualitative pattern.

\subsection{Bootstrap inference}

Naive standard errors are likely conservative for PSM and IPW (which require bootstrap inference for proper coverage) and approximately correct for DR. For publication-quality inference we recommend $B = 500$ bootstrap iterations. The substantive convergence across estimators is unlikely to be sensitive to this refinement.

\subsection{Unobserved selection}

All eleven estimators address selection on observables. They cannot account for selection on unobservables. Addressing this would require either an instrumental variables strategy or a Heckman-style selection model \citep{Heckman1979} with an exclusion restriction.

\subsection{Measurement error in event timing}

The IEA database does not record exact event dates; we assign events to the midpoint between announcement and expected commissioning. This measurement error attenuates time-varying covariate effects but is unlikely to bias the $\BlueCCS$ coefficient, since technology is fixed at announcement.

\subsection{Leave-one-region-out robustness}

To test whether the $\BlueCCS$ finding is driven by a particular region, we re-estimate the hazard model excluding each of the seven regions in turn. Across all seven leave-one-region-out (LOR) specifications, the $\BlueCCS$ coefficient remains statistically significant ($p < 0.0001$ throughout) and large in magnitude (range $11.08$ to $19.89$ around the full-sample value of $14.32$).

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Leave-one-region-out robustness.}
\label{tab:lor}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Specification} & \textbf{$n$ proj.} & \textbf{$n$ events} & \textbf{$\BlueCCS$ HR} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Full sample} & $714$ & $43$ & $\mathbf{14.32}$ & $[7.52, 27.25]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
Excl. Asia (0 events) & $608$ & $43$ & $14.32$ & $[7.52, 27.26]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
Excl. EU & $501$ & $37$ & $14.64$ & $[6.45, 33.26]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
Excl. North America & $552$ & $29$ & $13.34$ & $[8.55, 20.82]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
Excl. MENA & $695$ & $41$ & $12.93$ & $[6.48, 25.79]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
Excl. Other & $638$ & $40$ & $11.08$ & $[5.06, 24.28]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
Excl. Other Europe & $627$ & $32$ & $18.06$ & $[10.37, 31.46]$ & $<0.0001$ \\
