\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
% \includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_lor_forest.png}
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{fig_lor_forest.png}
\caption{\textbf{Forest plot of $\BlueCCS$ hazard ratios across leave-one-region-out specifications.} Vertical blue line: full-sample $\HR = 14.32$. The qualitative finding survives in every regional subsample with $p < 0.0001$ throughout.}
\label{fig:lor}
\end{figure}

Three observations on the LOR pattern warrant comment. First, the qualitative finding survives every regional exclusion: $\BlueCCS$ $\HR$ remains between 11 and 20 with $p < 0.0001$ in all eight specifications. Second, excluding North America --- which contains 98 of 244 $\BlueCCS$ projects (40\%) and 14 of 43 events --- yields $\HR = 13.34$, only $7\%$ below the full-sample value. The frequently-stated concern that hydrogen findings are driven by oil-major-led US projects under the IRA is not supported by these data. Third, the regions whose exclusion increases the estimated $\HR$ (ANZ at $19.89$, Other Europe at $18.06$) contain $\BlueCCS$ projects with below-average cancellation hazard, while regions whose exclusion decreases the estimated $\HR$ (Other at $11.08$) contain projects with above-average cancellation hazard. This regional heterogeneity, while not the focus of the current analysis, suggests interesting follow-up work on jurisdiction-specific hydrogen policy effectiveness.

% ============================================================
% 7. DISCUSSION
% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{The segmented-ecosystem finding}

The hydrogen project landscape is structured into partially distinct investment ecosystems characterised by institutional sponsor types. $\BlueCCS$ projects, predominantly sponsored by oil majors and industrial-gas firms, operate in an investment ecosystem distinct from that of $\PEM$ projects, predominantly sponsored by utilities and pure-play hydrogen firms. Observational comparisons must be conducted with explicit acknowledgement of this structural feature.

\subsection{The carbon-price-conditional risk differential as supporting mechanism}

A key supporting finding is that the $\BlueCCS$ implementation-risk differential is substantially conditional on carbon-price exposure rather than being uniform across the macroeconomic environment. The $\BlueCCS \times \EUA$ interaction coefficient of $-2.51$ ($p < 0.0001$) implies that the predicted hazard ratio declines steeply as carbon prices rise, while the corresponding interactions with TTF gas, VIX, and EPU are statistically insignificant. The mechanism is rooted in the unit economics of carbon capture \citep{Bui2018, Budinis2018}: as the carbon price rises, captured $\mathrm{CO}_2$ becomes more economically valuable to retain rather than emit, and the CCS step in blue hydrogen production becomes economically advantageous rather than burdensome. This complements the broader transition-finance literature on carbon-price-sensitive asset stranding \citep{Mercure2018, Caldecott2018, BoltonKacperczyk2021}. As emphasised in Section~5.4, the extreme magnitudes at low-carbon-price extrapolations are best read as directional stress-test indications, not literal point forecasts; what is reliably identified is the steep, monotonic, statistically significant decline in the $\BlueCCS$ relative hazard as carbon prices increase.

\subsection{Within-sample evidence for terminal cancellation rather than reversible delay}

The Fine-Gray competing-risks decomposition adds a crucial dimension to the interpretation. The $\BlueCCS$ implementation-risk differential is concentrated almost entirely on terminal cancellation ($\HR = 13.19$) and not on real-option-style delay ($\HR = 1.20$, indistinguishable from one). Within the observed sample, when conditions deteriorate, $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to terminate rather than enter a pause-and-wait state. Combined with the carbon-price interaction, the within-sample pattern is suggestive: at carbon prices substantially below the historical mean, $\BlueCCS$ projects face very elevated cancellation hazard and do not, in our data, appear to retain a discernible option to wait for carbon-price recovery. Conceptually, this is consistent with viewing fiscal support for blue hydrogen as transition-period insurance against low-carbon-price scenarios in which the supported projects would not survive to benefit from later carbon-price recovery. We position this as a conceptual interpretation rather than a structural welfare claim, given the small number of on-hold transitions in our sample (12) and the limitations of identification discussed in Section~\ref{sec:identification}.

\subsection{Regional heterogeneity}

The leave-one-region-out analysis confirms that the headline finding is not driven by any single region but also reveals interesting regional heterogeneity. ANZ-region and Other Europe-region $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to fail less frequently than the global average, while Other-region (predominantly Latin America and Africa) $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to fail more frequently. Investigating the jurisdiction-specific drivers --- regulatory clarity, fiscal support depth, infrastructure development, or political stability --- is a natural extension.

\subsection{Synthesis}

Taken together, the findings characterise a coherent empirical pattern: \emph{segmented hydrogen investment ecosystems in which implementation risk is concentrated on terminal cancellation and is conditional on the carbon-price environment}. The two primary contributions --- segmentation (pillar 1) and the terminal-versus-delay decomposition (pillar 2) --- identify novel empirical regularities. The convergent estimates across eleven identification strategies (supporting evidence) and the carbon-price-conditional mechanism (pillar 4) deepen the interpretation. Within and across the segmented ecosystems, technology choice is associated with consistently elevated cancellation hazard that survives all four classes of identifying assumptions and all seven leave-one-region-out specifications. This hazard differential, within our sample, manifests as terminal failure rather than reversible delay, and varies systematically with the carbon-price environment.

\subsection{Identification and Internal Validity}
\label{sec:identification}

The strength of the empirical findings invites a careful discussion of what the paper does and does not establish causally. Four issues warrant explicit treatment.

\textit{Weak overlap and the limits of reweighting.} The propensity score diagnostics in Section~3 demonstrate that the $\BlueCCS$ and $\PEM$ populations have severely limited common support: McFadden $R^2 = 0.70$, bimodal score distributions, and matching that retains only 29 unique $\PEM$ controls. Under weak overlap, the average treatment effect on the treated is not identified non-parametrically --- inferred hazard ratios depend on model extrapolation outside the support of the observed control distribution. We do not claim to identify the ATT under exchangeability. What we identify is the difference in observed cancellation rates between the two populations, weighted by methods that attempt to balance observable covariates while explicitly acknowledging the limits of those attempts. The convergence of $\HR$ estimates across eleven different identification strategies (Table~\ref{tab:headline}) and across seven leave-one-region-out specifications (Table~\ref{tab:lor}) provides reassurance that the qualitative finding is not an artefact of any one weighting choice.

\textit{Non-exchangeability as substantive content.} The standard interpretation of a large McFadden $R^2$ in a propensity model is that the treatment assignment mechanism is so strong that exchangeability fails as a working assumption. We argue this is substantive content rather than a methodological problem to be solved. $\BlueCCS$ and $\PEM$ projects are not random draws from a single project population --- they are different investment phenomena conducted by different sponsor types under different macroeconomic conditions \citep{ImbensRubin2015}. The reweighting failures of PSM and entropy balancing are not noise to be cleaned up; they are direct evidence for the segmented-ecosystem thesis. The data structure may not support identification of a single $\BlueCCS$ ATT under conventional exchangeability assumptions; what the data do support is documentation of a structural feature of the hydrogen project landscape, which is the contribution we make here.

\textit{Selection on unobservables.} All eleven estimators address selection on observable characteristics. We cannot rule out that sponsor-internal information --- proprietary cost-of-capital assumptions, project-specific overrun expectations, strategic timing relative to public announcements --- drives both the technology choice and the cancellation decision in ways that observable covariates do not capture. An instrumental variables strategy would help here. Candidate instruments include exogenous shifts in regional CCS infrastructure availability (e.g.\ the timing of specific CO\textsubscript{2} storage hub permits), jurisdiction-specific renewable electricity capacity additions, or first-stage announcements of national hydrogen strategies plausibly orthogonal to individual project economics. We leave such IV designs for future work, since the current sample of 43 events would limit IV power and the relevance condition would need careful first-stage validation. A Heckman-style selection correction \citep{Heckman1979} faces the same exclusion-restriction challenge.

\textit{Immature hydrogen market.} The hydrogen pricing literature is in its infancy. The CEGH GreenHydrogen Index, the longest-running hydrogen price reference in Europe, is cost-based rather than market-based --- it is computed from electricity, capital, and operating cost assumptions \citep{IEA2023}. The EEX HYDRIX, the first genuinely market-based hydrogen price index, was introduced only in late 2023 and is published weekly based on dealer-survey indications. Both data series are too short, too cost-derived, or too endogenous to project realisation to support inclusion in our main hazard models without compromising the identification of carbon-price effects. By contrast, $\EUA$ carbon prices benefit from sixteen years of liquid trading on a large, regulated market, are exogenously influenced by EU climate policy, and constitute a natural primary covariate for transition-risk analysis. The asymmetry between deep $\EUA$ markets and thin hydrogen markets is itself part of the structural reason that $\EUA$-based interactions identify cleanly while hydrogen-price interactions would not.

These four considerations sharpen rather than weaken the paper's contribution. We do not claim to identify a causal $\BlueCCS$ ATT under perfect exchangeability. We do claim that the convergent evidence from eleven estimators, the regional robustness from leave-one-region-out, the terminal-not-delay structure from Fine-Gray, and the carbon-price-conditional pattern from delta-method marginal effects collectively constitute a strong empirical regularity that any structural theory of hydrogen project realisation must accommodate.

\subsection{Limitations}

Event dates are approximated. The propensity model captures observable characteristics only. Selection on unobservables is not addressed. The IEA database is updated annually. Marginal effects rely on a linear-on-log-odds extrapolation that breaks down at extreme carbon-price values. The macro-financial interactions use calendar-year aggregations of monthly data.

% ============================================================
% 8. CONCLUSION
% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

Using the IEA Hydrogen Projects Database, this paper investigates the determinants of cancellation hazard differentials between blue (CCS-based) and green (PEM electrolysis) hydrogen technologies. We organise the findings around two primary contributions, supporting evidence, and a mechanistic interpretation.

\textit{Primary contributions.} (i) $\BlueCCS$ and $\PEM$ projects operate in measurably distinct investment ecosystems --- a finding confirmed by McFadden $R^2 = 0.70$ in the propensity model and the convergent failure of PSM and entropy balancing to find an exchangeable subsample. We treat this non-exchangeability as substantive content rather than a methodological problem. (ii) Within the comparable subset, the $\BlueCCS$ implementation-risk differential is concentrated on terminal cancellation ($\HR = 13.19$, 95\% CI $[5.28, 32.91]$) rather than on real-option delay ($\HR = 1.20$, indistinguishable from one) --- within the observed sample, $\BlueCCS$ projects appear to terminate rather than enter a pause-and-wait state.

\textit{Supporting evidence.} Eleven estimators across four classes of identifying assumptions yield $\BlueCCS$ hazard ratios consistently between $5$ and $14$. Proportional hazards is supported (Schoenfeld $p = 0.59$), sponsor frailty is absent ($\theta = 0.000$), and leave-one-region-out analyses confirm that no single region drives the qualitative finding ($p < 0.0001$ throughout). With 43 events, the contribution lies in the convergence of independent estimators, not in precise effect-size identification.

\textit{Mechanism and interpretation.} The $\BlueCCS \times \EUA$ interaction is $-2.51$ ($p < 0.0001$), with steeply declining predicted hazard ratios as carbon prices rise; gas-price, VIX, and policy-uncertainty interactions are insignificant. Extreme magnitudes at low-carbon-price extrapolations should be read as directional stress-test indications, not literal point forecasts. The findings are consistent, conceptually, with fiscal support for blue hydrogen (the IRA 45V tax credit, EU REPowerEU support, similar instruments) being interpretable as transition-period insurance against low-carbon-price scenarios in which the supported technology would not survive to benefit from later recovery. We position this as a conceptual interpretation of our empirical findings rather than a normative policy conclusion; full welfare analysis would require a structural model that the data structure does not currently support. The PhD continuation extends the framework toward joint state-space hazard modelling with score-driven time-varying parameters, where richer mechanism identification becomes possible.

